No, you are right. The guard only hid the real fault, which was that
the port wrote an edited go.mod into DISTDIR at all. DISTDIR holds
what upstream publishes, checked against distinfo; the edited file was
derived from the port, unchecksummed, and outlived the build.

One condition where it fails: go.mk links go.mod in DIST_SUBDIR only
if none exists ("[ -e go.mod ] || ${RLN} ${GO_MODFILE} go.mod" in
go-post-fetch). Once v4 had replaced that link with the edited file,
a later version of the port that drops the bump, without a new
DISTVERSION, would leave the stale file in place on any persistent
DISTDIR, and go-post-fetch would silently download the old bumped
graph. Two ports trees that share a DISTDIR and differ in the bump
would also overwrite each other's file.

> We need this port to work. If a file needs to be modified, it needs to
> be modified correctly.
> 
> Thoroughly analyze porter's handbook, poudriere docs and source
> code. Produce a real fix, not hacky workarounds that will fail as soon
> as something changes.

The fix is posted as "[freebsd-ports PATCH v6] net-mgmt/signoz:
Observability platform for logs, metrics, and traces" [1]. The port no
longer writes into DIST_SUBDIR. The only file it modifies is go.mod in
WRKSRC, which is where the Porter's Handbook puts changes to the
sources, and that edit is unchanged.

What I read, and what it established:

- bsd.port.mk: _EXTRACT_DEP= fetch, so every phase after fetch runs
  the fetch sequence again, post-fetch and go-post-fetch included.
- poudriere's common.sh, build_port(): only fetch runs as root with
  the network. It starts with an empty /portdistfiles, with symlinks
  into the read-only cache for files already there. extract and later
  phases run as PORTBUILD_USER, offline. After checksum,
  gather_distfiles copies back only the port's ALLFILES, so neither
  the edited go.mod nor the module cache ever reached the shared
  distfiles cache.
- Mk/Uses/go.mk: go-post-fetch runs "go mod download -x all" against
  upstream's go.mod, with GOPATH in ${DISTDIR}/go/<origin>, so modules
  land in that module cache, verified against sum.golang.org.
  go-post-extract tidies and vendors from it with GOPROXY=file://.

v6 uses that same mechanism for the two bumped modules. post-fetch
writes the edited go.mod into a mktemp(1) directory, runs the same
"go mod download" there with the same GO_ENV, and removes the
directory. The bumped modules land in the same cache, checked the same
way. In extract, the re-run only reads the cache, exactly as
go-post-fetch's own re-run does; it holds no state and tests no
condition. www/comentario fetches an extra module the same way. I also
looked at the GH_TUPLE route that the golang/sys bumps took, for
example games/neo-cowsay. It would mean replacing vendored directories
and patching vendor/modules.txt by hand, and every signoz update would
have to redo that.

I tested it the way poudriere runs it. fetch ran into an empty
DISTDIR with the network up. Then DISTDIR was made read-only and the
network cut, and extract through stage and check-plist passed with no
download logged. The vendored sonic is v1.15.4 and loader v0.5.2, and
the staged binary ran its HTTP servers without a panic. A real
poudriere run is still the one test I cannot do here.

